WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.640
Good to have you on models and agents, episode 123 for July 26th, 2026.

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:10.940
Plenty of model releases and agent benchmarks moving today.

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:17.360
Photon 1 pre-trains on raw video alone to simulate desktops and play checkers,

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:22.100
removing the action label requirement that has limited most video agents.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:30.620
Induction labs released a 106 billion A5B sparse mixture of experts imagination model called

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:36.400
Photon 1. It was pre-trained on raw video with no action labels at all.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.900
The model learns desktops simulation, checkers play, and billiard physics directly from passive

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:49.040
video. This removes the explicit action frame pairings that most prior video agents required.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:54.940
The test system targets the data bottleneck that constrained earlier video based agents.

00:00:55.640 --> 00:01:01.520
Builders working on long horizon agents or world models can now explore pre-training pipelines

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:07.900
that skip expensive action annotation. Watch for follow-up releases testing whether the same

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:14.520
label-free approach scales to more complex environments. In model updates, the Gemma 4 family has

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:21.420
now been downloaded more than 300 million times. The full Gemma open model series exceeds 900

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:27.920
million downloads. Deep-mind positions these releases as core contributions to an open ecosystem

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:36.160
alongside JX and Transformers. Demi's Hassabis noted that a strong open ecosystem supports responsible

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:43.220
deployment of both open and proprietary models. Builders evaluating open models for production can

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:48.700
factor these download figures into decisions about community support and fine-tuning momentum.

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:56.100
Sakana AI released Fugu Cyber, a security-tuned endpoint on its Fugu orchestration model.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:08.020
It reports 86.9% on CyberGym and 72.1% on CTI RAM. The scores edge pass GPT 5.5 Cyber and

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:14.560
Cloud Mythos preview on the same benchmarks. Access requires manual approval plus a defensive

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:21.820
use policy and token plan. Security teams evaluating orchestration models for endpoint tasks should

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:29.220
request access and run their own CyberGym evaluations before deployment. A bachelor's project implemented

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:37.920
full YOLO 26N inference in ARM 64 assembly plus C on Raspberry Pi 4. The work includes neon

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:46.940
siling winnograd convolution, optimized gem kernels, and custom micro kernels for CONV, C3K2, SPPF,

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:54.540
C2 PSA, and detect layers. The custom binary format and operator fusion produced correct detections,

00:02:54.540 --> 00:03:00.720
though measured speedups fell short of initial targets. Edge developers targeting ARM devices

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:06.840
without full frameworks can study the released repository for memory layout and cache tiling patterns.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:14.020
Signal pilot labs evaluated Frontier and sub Frontier models on the 2026 International

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:21.520
Mathematical Olympiad problems, using multiple harnesses including Autofine. Frontier models reached

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:28.680
perfect or near perfect scores regardless of harness. Sonnet, Opus, and GLM4 improved with the

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:35.260
multi-agent harness, but still trailed. Every sub Frontier model missed the key reduction on the hardest

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:43.260
problem P3, even in 20 hour runs that proved all other problems. Grading combined a separate Frontier

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:50.660
model with manual verification by former IMO medallists. Teams building math or reasoning agents should

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:56.140
test whether their current harness supplies the missing high-level idea generation, rather than

00:03:56.140 --> 00:04:05.020
only retrieval and verification. Rough 0.16.0 raised the number of default-enabled rules from 59 to

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:15.120
413. It flagged 1,618 issues across E-squalite-yudels alone. The update also expanded the total

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:23.780
rule count from 708 to 968 since the last default change. Simon Willison used UVX rough at

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:30.040
latest check dot with fix and unsafe fixes to auto-resolve the bulk of issues in data set,

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:37.580
ski light, hyphen utels, and LLM. Roughly 80 issues per project remained for manual review,

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:45.540
including DTC-005 daytime calls without time zone arguments. Python developers should run the

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:51.820
latest rough with unsafe fixes on their largest repositories this week, and review the remaining

00:04:51.820 --> 00:05:00.160
issues for manual attention. Reactor released Open Dreamer, a JX-Flax NNX reproduction of the Dreamer

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:07.200
4 world model pipeline that includes the full training recipe. The repositories provide a causal

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:14.000
video tokenizer, action-conditioned latent dynamics model, rollout generation, and FVD scoring.

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:20.100
Two separate repos were published, with next state Open Dreamer holding the training pipeline.

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:26.060
Researchers reproducing world models can now start from the published training code instead of

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:32.580
re-implementing the pipeline from papers. Okay, let's pop the hood on vector search indexes.

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:39.540
In memory, HNSW delivers single-digit millisecond queries, but requires the entire graph to

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:45.940
fit in RAM. Teams quickly hit expensive instance sizes once collections exceed a few hundred million

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:53.320
vectors. Disk-based approaches, such as span and disk A&amp;N, keep only a small hot set in memory,

00:05:53.940 --> 00:06:00.300
and fetch compressed neighbor lists from SSD. This adds roughly 10 to 30 milliseconds per query,

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:06.860
while cutting RAM needs by five to ten times on the same data set. The quality gap appears mainly

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:13.480
at high recall targets above 95%, where disk methods need extra re-ranking passes.

00:06:14.260 --> 00:06:19.860
When your working set stays under roughly 50 million vectors and sub-ten millisecond latency

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:26.700
matters more than cost, stay in RAM. Once collections grow larger or you need to serve many tenants on

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:32.560
the same hardware. Move to a disk-aware index and budget for the extra re-ranking step.

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:40.040
The gacha that bites most teams is underestimating how quickly re-ranking cost grows once recall targets

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:50.200
push past 0.95 on billion scale collections. Run UVXRough at latestcheck.withfix and unsafe fixes on

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:55.460
your largest Python project to surface the new default rules before they break CI.

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:03.360
Request access to Fougu Cyber and run it on your own CyberGym subset to see whether the 86.9%

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:10.600
score holds on your security tasks. Clone the Open Dreamer repositories and train a small world model

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:19.500
on your own video data to test label-free pre-training. Study the ARM 64, YOLO 26, and repository if you

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:26.040
need to deploy detection without heavy frameworks on Raspberry Pi class hardware. More label-free

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:31.640
video foundation models are expected as teams replicate the Photon-1 pre-training approach.

00:07:32.600 --> 00:07:39.380
Sakana AI may expand the Fougu family beyond the security-tuned endpoint now under manual approval.

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:46.660
Additional IMO-style benchmarks from independent groups will likely appear as the 2026 problems

00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:53.280
circulate. Tomorrow, keep an eye on further rough rule expansions given Astral's recent pace of

00:07:53.280 --> 00:08:00.220
enabling previously opt-in checks. That wraps up today's AI briefing. Share this with a developer or

